gtk: vmlab: the guest's arrow drawn into the picture where the host's pointer is not on it

0004 puts the guest's arrow on the host's pointer, with no drawn copy in the picture. When
something inside the guest moved the arrow (Remote Desktop, an agent's injected input), or the
host's pointer was off the window, the window showed no arrow at all, where a monitor shows
it. Now the arrow is drawn into the picture at the place the guest gives (the driver gives the
image's top-left; the host's pointer is the tip, so the two compare through the hot spot):
- the host's pointer off the picture: the arrow is drawn;
- the host's pointer moved within the last 250 ms: it drives the arrow and wears it, as before
  (the guest's reports lag the pointer by a few ms, so they are not compared then);
- the host's pointer still for longer: it wears the arrow while the guest has the arrow under
  it (2 pixels either way); elsewhere the arrow is drawn and the host's pointer is blank.
On the Wayland sub-surface (0007) the arrow is blended into the frame buffer, and each buffer
copies the picture back over where it had the arrow: two 64x64 squares a move. Where GTK
draws the picture, the arrow is painted on it. VNC and screenshots are unchanged: the arrow
is only in the window.

